1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:08,520
Heat with oil and own a heat pump? One evaluation's advice: switch
the heat pump off on the coldest days and let the oil burner run.

2
00:00:09,033 --> 00:00:20,457
Its switch-off point was 8 degrees Fahrenheit in 2015 and 25 degrees the year
after: the outdoor temperature below which oil ran cheaper, not a saving figure.

3
00:00:21,467 --> 00:00:32,531
The line moved because it is set by that year's oil and electricity prices and by how the heat
pump performs in the cold; if oil gets dearer against electricity, the point may move lower.

4
00:00:33,033 --> 00:00:48,489
This is one evaluation of ductless mini-splits in Massachusetts and Rhode Island homes, and the advice is for oil-heated
homes only: against electric resistance it found the heat pump nearly always cheaper to run, and against propane, always.

5
00:00:49,500 --> 00:00:56,028
The evaluators allowed that not every household
wishes to think about which heater to run and when.

6
00:00:56,533 --> 00:01:04,453
If you heat with oil, check whether oil or the heat pump runs cheaper at
this year's prices before the cold comes, and expect the answer to move.

7
00:01:04,767 --> 00:01:10,023
Which homes a heat pump saves money in at
all is the full inquiry; see the main video.
